result:
### Rationale
Four knobs were measured on the r2 code with 3 interleaved repeats per size, then composed into one dispatcher. Two survived, plus one trivial extension.

- **(b) Table evict_last on the row path:** kept for R > 450k rows. It gives −1.3..−1.8% at 612 MB and −0.5..−0.7% at 2689 MB.
- **(c) Plain x loads:** kept only for the 134 MB workload (S ≤ 256, R ≥ 120k), where it gives −1.5% in every repeat.
- **(a) Pair path at L:** extended only to S ≤ 2048, which changes only the 354 MB workload and ties on the bench.
- **(d) Evict_first policy on x loads:** dropped. It costs +6..+17% at 36–322 MB, so it closes H16.

The composed kernel passes 16/16 on the bench, with bands S −0.3%, M −0.4% and L −0.7% against r2 in the same session. The predicted portal score is 0.6842 ± 0.0115, against 0.6827 for r2.

The one workload above +1% is 15.8 MB at +2.8% (4.5 vs 4.4 µs). It runs `rope_pair<128,false>`, which is identical to r2's pair kernel, so I read it as noise.

